# Configuration 8 -- three identical shops, one bay, not enough plate.
#
#                     +--> ShopA --\
#   Furnace -> Bay ---+--> ShopB ----> WidgetBay --> Depot
#                     +--> ShopC --/
#
# The furnace makes 1.667 plate/tick. Each shop wants 1.0. Two thirds of the
# demand cannot be met, and the storage has to decide whose. That decision is
# the entire configuration.
#
# This is experiment-02.md's complaint made concrete: four factories drawing
# from one train station, and factory #0 always served first because it
# happened to be built first. Under `index` -- v1's accidental rule -- ShopA
# runs flat out, ShopB gets the leftovers, and ShopC never runs at all. Not
# once, in either direction, forever. Under `round_robin` the same plant with
# the same numbers gives all three an equal share.
#
# Both are exact and both are deterministic. They are different games.
#
# Note that these have to be three classes rather than one class of three: a
# class is the set of machines the arbiter refuses to distinguish, and the
# whole point here is a plant where it does distinguish them. Written as
# `ShopA x3` they would be interchangeable and would share automatically --
# which is the other half of the same idea.

item IronPlate
item Widget

blueprint Contested {
    source  Furnace { produces 50 IronPlate every 30 ticks }

    storage Bay {
        capacity 200
        policy round_robin
    }

    process ShopA { consumes 30 IronPlate takes 30 ticks produces 10 Widget }
    process ShopB { consumes 30 IronPlate takes 30 ticks produces 10 Widget }
    process ShopC { consumes 30 IronPlate takes 30 ticks produces 10 Widget }

    storage WidgetBay { capacity 500 }
    sink    Depot     { consumes 10 Widget every 5 ticks }

    wire Furnace -> Bay
    wire Bay -> ShopA -> WidgetBay
    wire Bay -> ShopB -> WidgetBay
    wire Bay -> ShopC -> WidgetBay
    wire WidgetBay -> Depot
}

deploy 1 x Contested
